\begin{abstract}
We develop a combinatorial theory of \emph{prediction agreement} for models built by splicing components of two or more fine-tunes of a common architecture, and apply it to a causal layer-transplant measurement on a pair of sibling language models. Modelling a predictor as a function from a finite set of $N$ evaluation positions to a token alphabet and writing $\agr(f,g)$ for the fraction of positions on which two predictors coincide, we prove that agreement obeys the Hamming triangle inequality $\agr(f,g)+\agr(g,h)\le 1+\agr(f,h)$, the \emph{portability budget}. From it we derive a \emph{both-parents-collapse certificate}: any hybrid $H$ of parents $A,B$ emits tokens agreeing with neither parent on at least $\agr(A,B)-\min\{\agr(H,A),\agr(H,B)\}$ of the positions. The certificate is sharp, and converts the measured transplant profile---cross-parent baseline $\beta=0.8327$; hybrid agreements $0.5845$ and $0.5443$ after swapping the final two layers---into the conclusion that at least $28.84\%$ of positions carry behaviour belonging to neither parent, and hence that the hybrid is provably \emph{not} a position-wise selector between its parents. The matched-width control swapping two middle layers ($0.9635$ with the host) leaves the certificate vacuous, separating the two swap sites. We further prove a $1$-Lipschitz dose--response principle, forcing the two hybrids to differ by at least $0.3790$ in normalized Hamming distance---more than the parents' own separation $0.1673$; a two-sided dissociation between cross-entropy cost and top-1 agreement, with a reverse-Markov localisation theorem forcing at least $11.63\%$ of windows to individually absorb at least $0.2326$ nats of the measured $0.4652$-nat average degradation; and a complete theory of \emph{shared serving}. For $k$ fine-tunes pairwise agreeing at most $\beta$, a single shared model has mean agreement at most $(1+\beta)/2$; this pairwise ceiling is attained for $k=2$ by an explicit balanced compromise, up to $1/(2N)$. For $k\ge3$ a multiplicity (Plotkin-type) count yields $kM^{2}\le M+(k-1)\beta$, whose exact solution is the \emph{serving-capacity curve} $M^{*}(k)=\big(1+\sqrt{1+4k(k-1)\beta}\big)/(2k)$. The two bounds cross exactly at $k(1-\beta)=2$, a sharp phase transition; $\sqrt{\beta}\le M^{*}(k)\le\sqrt{\beta}+1/k$, so $M^{*}(k)\to\sqrt{\beta}$. At the measured $\beta$ this caps ceiling-efficient serving at eleven fine-tunes. Finally, a defect identity shows that families attaining the multiplicity bound are rigid and \emph{quantised}, with $M=c/k$ and $\beta=c(c-1)/(k(k-1))$ for an integer $c$, and a complete $c$-design realises every such pair, so the extremal serving values are exactly the quantised ones.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The engineering question}

A pretrained transformer is routinely specialised into several descendants: a chat-tuned variant, a code variant, a domain variant. When these must be served concurrently from limited accelerator memory, one would like to store one copy of the weights they can share, plus a small per-model remainder. The question of \emph{which parameters are shareable} is usually settled by heuristics. It admits, instead, a direct causal test: physically overwrite a contiguous block of one fine-tune's layers with the corresponding block of another's, leaving all other parameters untouched, and evaluate the resulting hybrid on held-out text.

\subsection{The measurement}

The experiment was performed on a base/instruct pair from one pretrained family, over twelve held-out windows at context length $512$, with deterministic forwards and restore-by-construction between arms. The parents agree on their top-1 next-token predictions at a fraction
\[
\beta \;=\; 0.8327,
\]
the \emph{cross-parent baseline}; their normalized Hamming distance is $1-\beta=0.1673$.

