Three pre-registered hypotheses were tested. \textbf{P1} (``a tail swap pulls the hybrid toward the donor'') was \emph{refuted}: the tail hybrids fall far below the cross-parent baseline against \emph{both} parents. \textbf{P2} (direction asymmetry) was confirmed. \textbf{P3} (hybrids remain functional, within half a nat) was confirmed. The bulk arm transplants at no measurable cost, and in one direction slightly improves the recipient.

\subsection{Contributions}

Section~\ref{sec:geom} proves the portability budget and the both-parents-collapse certificate, with sharpness and an exact realisation of the measured profile. Section~\ref{sec:cost} develops the cost side. Section~\ref{sec:dose} proves the Lipschitz dose--response principle and the causal separation of swap sites. Section~\ref{sec:share} treats shared serving. Section~\ref{sec:rigid} proves rigidity, quantisation, and the exact realisation of extremal values by complete designs.

\section{Agreement geometry}\label{sec:geom}

Throughout, $\Omega$ is a finite nonempty set of \emph{evaluation positions}, $N=|\Omega|$, and $\mathcal{Y}$ a finite token alphabet. A \emph{predictor} is a function $f:\Omega\to\mathcal{Y}$.

\begin{definition}[Agreement, disagreement, novelty]
For predictors $f,g$ set $\Ag(f,g)=\{x: f(x)=g(x)\}$ and $\Dis(f,g)=\{x: f(x)\ne g(x)\}$, and
\[
\agr(f,g)=\frac{|\Ag(f,g)|}{N},\qquad \dis(f,g)=\frac{|\Dis(f,g)|}{N}=1-\agr(f,g).
\]
For a hybrid $H$ and parents $A,B$, the \emph{novelty set} is $\Nov(H;A,B)=\{x: H(x)\ne A(x)\ \text{and}\ H(x)\ne B(x)\}$, with $\nu(H;A,B)=|\Nov(H;A,B)|/N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Portability budget]\label{thm:tri}
For all predictors $f,g,h$ on $\Omega$,
\[
\agr(f,g)+\agr(g,h)\ \le\ 1+\agr(f,h).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $f(x)\ne h(x)$ then $f(x)\ne g(x)$ or $g(x)\ne h(x)$, so $\Dis(f,h)\subseteq\Dis(f,g)\cup\Dis(g,h)$, whence $|\Dis(f,h)|\le|\Dis(f,g)|+|\Dis(g,h)|$. Substituting $|\Dis|=N-|\Ag|$ and dividing by $N$ gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Sharing gap]
$\operatorname{gap}(H;A,B):=1+\agr(A,B)-\big(\agr(H,A)+\agr(H,B)\big)$.
\end{definition}

By Theorem~\ref{thm:tri}, $\operatorname{gap}\ge0$, with equality exactly when the hybrid saturates the budget.

\begin{theorem}[Both-parents-collapse certificate]\label{thm:cert}
For all $H,A,B$,
\[
\nu(H;A,B)\ \ge\ \agr(A,B)-\min\{\agr(H,A),\ \agr(H,B)\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $A(x)=B(x)$ then either $H(x)=A(x)$, or $H(x)$ differs from $A(x)$ and hence also from $B(x)$. Thus $\Ag(A,B)\subseteq\Nov(H;A,B)\cup\Ag(H,A)$, so $|\Ag(A,B)|\le|\Nov(H;A,B)|+|\Ag(H,A)|$; divide by $N$. Exchanging $A$ and $B$ and using symmetry of $\nu$ gives the donor-side bound; take the better of the two.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]
The bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:cert} is attained: on a two-position index set with $A=B$ constant and $H$ differing at exactly one position, $\agr(A,B)-\agr(H,A)=\nu(H;A,B)=\tfrac12$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}[Parent selector]
$H$ is a \emph{parent selector} for $(A,B)$ if $H(x)\in\{A(x),B(x)\}$ for every $x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:sel}
If $H$ is a parent selector then $\max\{\agr(H,A),\agr(H,B)\}\ge\agr(A,B)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
A selector has $\nu=0$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:cert}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Measured tail arm, base $\leftarrow$ instruct]\label{thm:tail}
Suppose $\agr(A,B)\ge0.8327$, $\agr(H,A)\le0.5845$, $\agr(H,B)\le0.5443$. Then
\[
\nu(H;A,B)\ \ge\ 0.2884, \qquad \max\{\agr(H,A),\agr(H,B)\}+0.2482\ \le\ \agr(A,B),
\]
and consequently $H$ is not a parent selector.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Reverse direction]
If $\agr(A,B)\ge0.8327$, $\agr(H,A)\le0.5887$ and $\agr(H,B)\le0.6289$, then $\nu(H;A,B)\ge0.2038$ and the better agreement is at least $0.2038$ below the baseline.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The control does not collapse]
If $\agr(A,B)\le0.8327$ and $\agr(H,A)\ge0.9635$, then $\agr(A,B)-\max\{\agr(H,A),\agr(H,B)\}<0$: the certificate is vacuous and a selector explanation remains available.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The collapse is not arithmetically forced]\label{thm:slack}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:tail}, $\operatorname{gap}(H;A,B)\ge0.7039$.
\end{theorem}

The budget permitted an agreement sum of $1.8327$; the measurement delivered at most $1.1288$.

\begin{theorem}[Exact realisation of the measured profile]
Partition $N=10{,}000$ positions into five classes of sizes $5000,3327,845,443,385$ and define three predictors into a three-token alphabet so that on class $0$ the parents agree and the hybrid follows them; on class $1$ the parents agree and the hybrid is novel; on class $2$ the parents differ and the hybrid follows $A$; on class $3$ the parents differ and the hybrid follows $B$; on class $4$ the parents differ and the hybrid is novel. Then exactly
\[
\agr(A,B)=0.8327,\quad \agr(H,A)=0.5845,\quad \agr(H,B)=0.5443,\quad \nu(H;A,B)=0.3712 .
\]
\end{theorem}

Since $0.3712>0.2884>0$, the measurement is consistent and the certificate is non-vacuous on the realised profile.

\section{Cost, agreement, and where damage lives}\label{sec:cost}

\subsection{A margin audit of the transfer prediction}

A prior conditional result states that if the host and donor prefix representations at a position differ coordinatewise by at most $\varepsilon$ and the donor tail holds its top-1 decision with margin greater than $2\varepsilon$, the spliced model reproduces the donor's decision. Call such a position \emph{margin-certified} at drift $\varepsilon$: with $u_x$ the donor tail's scores on its own prefix, $v_x$ its scores on the host prefix and $d(x)$ the donor's decision,
\[
\forall j\ne d(x):\ u_x(d(x))-u_x(j)>2\varepsilon, \qquad \forall j:\ |u_x(j)-v_x(j)|\le\varepsilon.
\]

\begin{proposition}
At a margin-certified position the hybrid's strict top-1 choice equals the donor's.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The strict top of $u_x$ leads by more than $2\varepsilon$; a uniform $\varepsilon$-perturbation closes at most $2\varepsilon$ of any gap, so the same index is the strict top of $v_x$. Strict tops are unique.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Margin-failure fraction]
If the hybrid's decisions are strict tops and its agreement with the donor's decisions is at most $0.5443$, then the fraction of margin-uncertified positions is at least $0.4557$.
\end{theorem}

The prior prediction is thus not falsified \emph{as a theorem}; its hypothesis is falsified \emph{as a fact}. The final layers operate in a diffuse, low-margin decision regime, which is why their decisions do not travel.

\subsection{Zero cost does not certify agreement}

\begin{theorem}[Cost/agreement dissociation]
For every $t\in(0,\tfrac12)$ there exist strictly positive probability vectors $q_{1},q_{2}$ on two tokens with $\CE(p,q_{1})=\CE(p,q_{2})$ for $p=(\tfrac12,\tfrac12)$, where $\CE(p,q)=-\sum_i p_i\log q_i$, whose top-1 choices are opposite.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $q_{1}=(\tfrac12+t,\tfrac12-t)$ and $q_{2}=(\tfrac12-t,\tfrac12+t)$; both cross-entropies equal $-\tfrac12\log(\tfrac12+t)-\tfrac12\log(\tfrac12-t)$.
\end{proof}

Hence a vanishing $\Delta\CE$ licenses no inference about prediction agreement, and the $0.9635$ agreement measured in the bulk arm is independent evidence.

\begin{theorem}[The correct converse]
If $|\log q_{1}(i)-\log q_{2}(i)|\le\kappa$ for all $i$, then $|\CE(p,q_{1})-\CE(p,q_{2})|\le\kappa$ for every probability vector $p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The difference equals $\sum_i p_i\big(\log q_{2}(i)-\log q_{1}(i)\big)$; apply the triangle inequality termwise and $\sum_i p_i=1$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Macroscopic cost is locally concentrated}

\begin{theorem}[Reverse Markov / cost localisation]
Let $f:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}$ satisfy $f(x)\le C$ for all $x$ with $C>0$, and have mean at least $\Delta$. Then
\[
\frac{|\{x: f(x)\ge\Delta/2\}|}{N}\ \ge\ \frac{\Delta}{2C}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $\Delta\le0$ the claim is trivial. Otherwise split the sum over $S=\{f\ge\Delta/2\}$ and its complement: the heavy part contributes at most $|S|C$, the light part at most $N\Delta/2$. Since the total is at least $N\Delta$, $N\Delta/2\le|S|C$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
With the measured mean excess $\Delta=0.4652$ nats and a per-window cap $C=2$ nats, at least $11.63\%$ of held-out windows individually lose at least $0.2326$ nats.
\end{corollary}

\section{Dose--response and the separation of swap sites}\label{sec:dose}

\begin{theorem}[Lipschitz agreement and novelty]
For predictors $H_{1},H_{2}$ and any fixed $A$ and any fixed pair $A,B$,
\[
|\agr(H_{1},A)-\agr(H_{2},A)|\le\dis(H_{1},H_{2}),\qquad
|\nu(H_{1};A,B)-\nu(H_{2};A,B)|\le\dis(H_{1},H_{2}).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
In both cases the symmetric difference of the relevant sets is contained in $\Dis(H_{1},H_{2})$: altering the hybrid at one position can change membership only at that position.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Swap-site separation]
If $\agr(H_{\mathrm{bulk}},A)\ge0.9635$ and $\agr(H_{\mathrm{tail}},A)\le0.5845$, then $\dis(H_{\mathrm{bulk}},H_{\mathrm{tail}})\ge0.3790$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
If moreover $\agr(A,B)\ge0.8327$, then $\dis(A,B)<\dis(H_{\mathrm{bulk}},H_{\mathrm{tail}})$: two transplants of the same parent pair are further from each other ($\ge0.3790$) than the parents are ($0.1673$).
\end{corollary}

\section{Shared serving: ceiling, attainment, capacity}\label{sec:share}

\begin{theorem}[Pairwise ceiling]\label{thm:ceil}
Let $A_{1},\dots,A_{k}$ with $k\ge2$ satisfy $\agr(A_{i},A_{j})\le\beta$ for all $i\ne j$. Then for any $H$,
\[
\sum_{i=1}^{k}\agr(H,A_{i})\ \le\ \frac{k(1+\beta)}{2},\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad M:=\frac1k\sum_{i=1}^{k}\agr(H,A_{i})\ \le\ \frac{1+\beta}{2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:tri}, $\agr(H,A_{i})+\agr(H,A_{j})\le1+\beta$ for each ordered pair $i\ne j$. Summing over all $k(k-1)$ ordered pairs, the left side is $2(k-1)\sum_i\agr(H,A_i)$ and the right is $k(k-1)(1+\beta)$; divide by $2(k-1)>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The bulk arm is near-optimal sharing]
If $\agr(A,B)\le0.8327$, $\agr(H,A)\ge0.9635$ and $\agr(H,B)\ge0.8385$, then $0\le\operatorname{gap}(H;A,B)\le0.0307$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The sharing boundary, quantified]
With the measured bulk and tail arms on the same parent pair,
\[
22\cdot\operatorname{gap}(H_{\mathrm{bulk}};A,B)\ \le\ \operatorname{gap}(H_{\mathrm{tail}};A,B).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine the previous theorem with Theorem~\ref{thm:slack}: $22\times0.0307=0.6754\le0.7039$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The ceiling is attained by a balanced compromise}

\begin{theorem}[Balanced attainment]\label{thm:bal}
For any parents $A,B$ on a nonempty $\Omega$ there exists $H$ with
\[
\agr(H,A)+\agr(H,B)=1+\agr(A,B) \qquad\text{and}\qquad |\agr(H,A)-\agr(H,B)|\le\frac1N .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $D=\Dis(A,B)$ and $S\subseteq D$ with $|S|=\lfloor|D|/2\rfloor$. Put $H=B$ on $S$ and $H=A$ off $S$. Then $\Dis(H,A)=S$ and $\Dis(H,B)=D\setminus S$, so
\[
|\Ag(H,A)|+|\Ag(H,B)|=(N-|S|)+(N-|D|+|S|)=N+|\Ag(A,B)| .
\]
The two agreement counts differ by $|D|-2|S|\in\{0,1\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Optimal balanced sharing]
There is $H$ with $\min\{\agr(H,A),\agr(H,B)\}\ge\frac{1+\agr(A,B)}{2}-\frac{1}{2N}$, and every $K$ satisfies $\min\{\agr(K,A),\agr(K,B)\}\le\frac{1+\agr(A,B)}{2}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[What the tail swap forfeits]
If $\agr(A,B)\ge0.8327$ and $\agr(H_{\mathrm{tail}},B)\le0.5443$, there is a shared model $H$ with
\[
\min\{\agr(H,A),\agr(H,B)\}-\min\{\agr(H_{\mathrm{tail}},A),\agr(H_{\mathrm{tail}},B)\}\ \ge\ 0.372-\frac{1}{2N}.
\]
\end{theorem}

A balanced shared model holds $0.9163$ with \emph{both} fine-tunes at once, while the tail hybrid holds at most $0.5443$ with its donor. Measured in \emph{mean} agreement across the two parents---half the sharing gap---the bulk transplant reaches $0.9010$, within $0.0154$ of the optimum $0.9163$, whereas the tail transplant reaches only $0.5644$.

\subsection{More than two fine-tunes}

Let $n(x)=\#\{i: H(x)=A_{i}(x)\}$ be the number of fine-tunes matched at position $x$.

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicity, Plotkin-type bound]\label{thm:mult}
With $s=\sum_{i}\agr(H,A_{i})$ and pairwise agreement at most $\beta$,
\[
s^{2}\ \le\ s+k(k-1)\beta,\qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad kM^{2}\ \le\ M+(k-1)\beta .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Double counting gives $\sum_{x}n(x)=sN$ and $\sum_{x}n(x)^{2}=\sum_{i,j}|\Ag(H,A_{i})\cap\Ag(H,A_{j})|$. Diagonal terms contribute $sN$; each off-diagonal term is at most $|\Ag(A_{i},A_{j})|\le\beta N$, since a position matched for both $i$ and $j$ is one where $A_i$ and $A_j$ agree. Cauchy--Schwarz gives $(sN)^{2}\le N\sum_{x}n(x)^{2}\le N\big(sN+k(k-1)\beta N\big)$; divide by $N^{2}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict decay above threshold]
If $k(1-\beta)>2$ then $M<(1+\beta)/2$ strictly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Capacity bound]
If $M\ge(1+\beta)/2$ then $k(1-\beta)\le2$, i.e.\ $k\le2/(1-\beta)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness: the hub family]\label{thm:hub}
For every $k\ge2$ there are $k$ predictors on a $k$-position index set with pairwise agreement exactly $\beta=1-2/k$, and a shared model whose agreement with each equals $(1+\beta)/2=1-1/k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Positions $\{1,\dots,k\}$, tokens $\{0,1\}$; let $A_{i}$ be the indicator of $\{i\}$ and $H\equiv0$. Then $A_{i}$ and $A_{j}$ differ exactly at $i$ and $j$, and $H$ differs from $A_{i}$ only at $i$.
\end{proof}

At $\beta=0.8327$ one has $2/(1-\beta)=11.95\ldots$: at most eleven fine-tunes can be served at the pairwise ceiling; twelve already force $M<0.91635$, and a hundred force $M\le0.913$.

\subsection{The serving-capacity curve}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{serving-capacity curve} is the positive root of $kx^{2}-x-(k-1)\beta$:
\[
M^{*}(k,\beta):=\frac{1+\sqrt{1+4k(k-1)\beta}}{2k}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact solution]
For $k\ge2$ and pairwise agreement at most $\beta$, every shared model satisfies $M\le M^{*}(k,\beta)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Attainment]
At $\beta=1-2/k$ one has $M^{*}(k,\beta)=1-1/k=(1+\beta)/2$, realised exactly by the hub family of Theorem~\ref{thm:hub}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exact crossing / phase transition]\label{thm:cross}
For $k\ge1$ and $\beta\ge0$: if $k(1-\beta)\ge2$ then $M^{*}(k,\beta)\le(1+\beta)/2$, strictly when $k(1-\beta)>2$; and if $\beta<1$ with $k(1-\beta)<2$ then $(1+\beta)/2<M^{*}(k,\beta)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both cases reduce, after clearing the square root, to the sign of $\big(k(1-\beta)\big)\big(k(1-\beta)-2\big)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sandwich and limit]
For $k\ge1$ and $0\le\beta\le1$,
\[
\sqrt{\beta}\ \le\ M^{*}(k,\beta)\ \le\ \sqrt{\beta}+\frac1k, \qquad\text{hence}\qquad \lim_{k\to\infty}M^{*}(k,\beta)=\sqrt{\beta}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
With $\sigma=\sqrt{\beta}$ one checks $(2k\sigma-1)^{2}\le1+4k(k-1)\beta\le(2k\sigma+1)^{2}$ using $k\sigma(1-\sigma)\ge0$.
\end{proof}

By AM--GM, $\sqrt{\beta}<(1+\beta)/2$ for $\beta<1$: the asymptotic serving value is the \emph{geometric} mean of the pairwise budget. At $\beta=0.8327$ the asymptote is $0.91252\ldots$, and the exact curve gives $M\le0.91634$ at $k=12$ and $M\le0.91297$ at $k=100$.

\section{Rigidity, quantisation, complete designs}\label{sec:rigid}

\begin{definition}
With $n(x)$ as above set
\[
\spread:=\tfrac12\sum_{x}\sum_{y}\big(n(x)-n(y)\big)^{2}, \qquad
\pairOverlap:=\sum_{i\ne j}\big|\Ag(H,A_{i})\cap\Ag(H,A_{j})\big| .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Defect identity]\label{thm:defect}
For all $H$, $(A_{i})$ and $\beta$,
\[
N^{2}\Big(s^{2}-s-k(k-1)\beta\Big)\;=\;-\spread\;-\;N\Big(k(k-1)\beta N-\pairOverlap\Big).
\]
\end{theorem}

Both subtracted terms are nonnegative, which reproves Theorem~\ref{thm:mult} and shows equality holds \emph{iff} both vanish.

\begin{definition}
$(H,(A_{i}))$ \emph{saturates} at budget $\beta$ if $s^{2}=s+k(k-1)\beta$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Constant multiplicity]
If $(H,(A_{i}))$ saturates then $n(x)=n(y)$ for all $x,y$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Pairwise tightness]
If $(H,(A_{i}))$ saturates then for all $i\ne j$, $\agr(A_{i},A_{j})=\beta$ and $\Ag(H,A_{i})\cap\Ag(H,A_{j})=\Ag(A_{i},A_{j})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Quantisation]\label{thm:quant}
If $(H,(A_{i}))$ saturates at budget $\beta$ with $k\ge2$, there is an integer $c\le k$ with
\[
M=\frac{c}{k},\qquad \beta=\frac{c(c-1)}{k(k-1)} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By constant multiplicity the matched count is a constant $c$; $\sum_{x}n(x)=sN$ gives $s=c$, so $M=c/k$. Substituting $s=c$ into $s^{2}=s+k(k-1)\beta$ yields $\beta=c(c-1)/(k(k-1))$.
\end{proof}

The hub family is the case $c=k-1$. Two immediate consequences: no family saturates at an irrational budget; and no family of twelve fine-tunes saturates at $\beta=0.8327$, since $c(c-1)=132\cdot 0.8327$ has no integer solution.

\begin{theorem}[Complete $c$-designs realise every quantised pair]\label{thm:design}
Let $2\le c\le k$. Take the positions to be all $c$-element subsets $S\subseteq\{1,\dots,k\}$, so $N=\binom{k}{c}$; let fine-tune $i$ predict a neutral token at $S$ when $i\in S$ and its own private token otherwise; let the shared model predict the neutral token everywhere. Then
\[
\agr(H,A_{i})=\frac{\binom{k-1}{c-1}}{\binom{k}{c}}=\frac{c}{k},\qquad
\agr(A_{i},A_{j})=\frac{\binom{k-2}{c-2}}{\binom{k}{c}}=\frac{c(c-1)}{k(k-1)}\ (i\ne j),
\]
and the family saturates at $\beta=c(c-1)/(k(k-1))$ with $M=c/k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exact classification]
For $k\ge2$, a pair $(\beta,M)$ is realised by a family attaining the multiplicity bound if and only if $(\beta,M)=\big(c(c-1)/(k(k-1)),\,c/k\big)$ for an integer $2\le c\le k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Designs sit on the capacity curve]
$M^{*}\!\big(k,\ c(c-1)/(k(k-1))\big)=c/k$, attained by the complete $c$-design.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The discriminant becomes $1+4k(k-1)\cdot\frac{c(c-1)}{k(k-1)}=(2c-1)^{2}$, so the root is $\big(1+(2c-1)\big)/(2k)=c/k$.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the measurement establishes}

First, a refutation with a certificate: the measured agreements force at least $28.84\%$ of positions to carry behaviour belonging to neither parent, and rule out any position-wise selector explanation. The tail carries no portable identity; it is entangled with upstream statistics. Second, a control that separates sites: the matched-width bulk arm is consistent with a selector, sits within $0.0307$ of the shared-serving optimum, and transplants at essentially zero cost, while the tail arm wastes at least $22\times$ as much of the sharing budget. Third, a dose--response geometry: the measured agreements force the two hybrids at least $0.3790$ apart, exceeding the parents' own separation $0.1673$.

\subsection{Practical guidance}

For multi-fine-tune serving on constrained memory: \emph{share everything except the last two layers; re-run the tail per model; do not approximate or borrow it}. Do not infer agreement from cost---the two are dissociable in both directions. Expect localised, not diffuse, damage from a bad splice. Budget capacity in advance: with fine-tunes as similar as this pair, one shared model serves at most eleven at the pairwise ceiling, after which the mean agreement decays along $M^{*}(k,\beta)$ toward $\sqrt{\beta}$.

\subsection{Scope and limitations}

The geometric results are unconditional statements about functions on a finite index set and are architecture-independent. The empirical inputs are narrower: a single fine-tune pair; twelve held-out windows at context length $512$; half-precision forwards; layer-pair granularity. Cross-entropy accounting matches the evaluation harness semantics, forwards are deterministic, and each arm restores the host by construction. No training is involved. The distinctive contribution relative to layer-amputation and model-merging work is the fine-tune-pair portability asymmetry at matched architecture, together with the both-parents-collapse signature and its certificate.

\subsection{Relation to coding theory}

Theorem~\ref{thm:mult} is a Plotkin-type bound proved by the classical second-moment argument. What is new is the exact solution of the resulting quadratic, the identification of its crossing with the triangle-inequality bound as a genuine phase transition (Theorem~\ref{thm:cross}), the defect identity (Theorem~\ref{thm:defect}) and resulting rigidity, and the exact classification of extremal configurations by complete designs (Theorem~\ref{thm:design}).

\section{Future directions}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Dose--response.} One- and three-layer swaps, tracing the curve whose Lipschitz envelope Section~\ref{sec:dose} supplies.
\item \textbf{Swap plus recalibration.} How much tail damage survives a cheap post-splice recalibration? This measures entanglement depth.
\item \textbf{Scale.} A $1.5$B parameter pair: does the boundary stay at two layers, or scale with depth?
\item \textbf{Quantised tails.} Does a compensated $4$-bit tail remain personal?
\item \textbf{Below the threshold.} Which $(\beta,M)$ with $k(1-\beta)<2$ are achievable?
\item \textbf{Approximate rigidity.} A stability version of the exact-equality rigidity theorems, usable on noisy data.
\item \textbf{Incomplete designs.} Which designs other than the complete $c$-design saturate, and what is the minimum number of positions realising a given quantised pair?
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

Prediction agreement between models is Hamming geometry, and Hamming geometry is unforgiving. A hybrid cannot be near two distant parents; agreement lost against the parents' consensus reappears as novelty; and a shared model serving many fine-tunes is limited first by a triangle-inequality ceiling and then, past a sharp threshold, by a multiplicity bound whose exact solution decays to the geometric mean of the pairwise budget. Against this backdrop the transplant measurement reads cleanly: the middle of the stack is common property, transplantable at no measurable cost and within three percentage points of the theoretical optimum for shared serving; the last two layers are load-bearing, and moving them produces a model that is a stranger to both parents on more than a quarter of its predictions.

